 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** $O(n)$ where $n$ is the number of voxels in the bounding box, as each voxel requires a constant-time index calculation and bit set operation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V/8)$ bytes where $V$ is the total number of voxels in the grid, as each voxel is stored as a single bit [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Bit packing significantly reduces memory footprint compared to byte-per-voxel storage. Parallel atomic writes can achieve high throughput on multi-core CPUs and GPUs [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Extremely compact storage for binary occupancy data; simple indexing scheme; efficient for sparse data if combined with sparse access patterns.
-*   **Limitations:** Not suitable for storing per-voxel attributes (e.g., density, color) without additional structures; random access to specific voxels requires bit manipulation; not compressed for runs of zeros/ones like RLE.
-*   **When to Use:** When memory bandwidth is a bottleneck and only binary occupancy is needed; when parallel voxelization is required.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When per-voxel attributes are needed; when the grid is extremely sparse and RLE or sparse octrees are more efficient.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V/64)$ bytes where $V$ is the total number of voxels in the grid, as each voxel is stored as a single bit packed into 64-bit integers [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Extremely compact storage for binary occupancy data; simple indexing scheme; efficient for sparse data if combined with sparse access patterns [G].
+*   **Limitations:** Not suitable for storing per-voxel attributes without additional structures; random access requires bit manipulation; not compressed for runs of zeros/ones like RLE [G].
+*   **When to Use:** When memory bandwidth is a bottleneck and only binary occupancy is needed; when parallel voxelization is required [G].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When per-voxel attributes are needed; when the grid is extremely sparse and RLE or sparse octrees are more efficient [G].
 
 ## Background
-Voxel indexing and bit compression is a fundamental technique in voxel-based rendering and geometry processing. It is widely used in GPU-accelerated volume rendering and mesh voxelization pipelines. The method is described in various implementations, including the `topskychen/voxelizer` repository [S1, unofficial], which uses atomic operations for parallel voxelization. The technique is also referenced in research on multiresolution MIP rendering [S2] and in the O-Voxel library for GPU-based volume rendering [S3, unofficial].
